(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :fire-weapon :discharge-weapon :set-trap :spring-trap :make-kill-decision :make-harvest-timing-decision)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2791003351-cloud-itonami-isco-3351-customs-border-inspectors-coverage" "2799007111-cloud-itonami-isco-7111-house-builders-coverage" "2799009311-cloud-itonami-isco-9311-mining-quarrying-labourers-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "fire the weapon" "fired the weapon" "fires the weapon" "firing the weapon" "fire a weapon" "fired a weapon" "fires a weapon" "firing a weapon" "discharge the weapon" "discharged the weapon" "discharges the weapon" "discharging the weapon" "discharge a weapon" "discharged a weapon" "discharges a weapon" "discharging a weapon" "set the trap" "sets the trap" "setting the trap" "set a trap" "sets a trap" "setting a trap" "spring the trap" "sprang the trap" "springs the trap" "springing the trap" "sprung the trap" "spring a trap" "sprang a trap" "springs a trap" "springing a trap" "make the kill decision" "made the kill decision" "makes the kill decision" "making the kill decision" "make the harvest-timing decision" "made the harvest-timing decision" "makes the harvest-timing decision" "making the harvest-timing decision")), (:adr/id 2799006224), (:actor/domain :agriculture-natural-resources/hunting-trapping-logistics-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-harvest-record :schedule-equipment-operation :flag-compliance-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :operator-verified :permit-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion :harvest-report-attached)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "6224"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-compliance-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799006224: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 6224 Hunters and Trappers logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 6224\n-- Hunters and Trappers** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of the\nrollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3 batches 1-3 of ~17 already\nlanded).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"6224\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/\nget-occupation \\\"6224\\\"))\"`), and its `:name` read exactly\n\"Hunters and Trappers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior\nto this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6224` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`6224` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nHunters and Trappers use firearms/traps and directly kill/capture wild\nanimals -- a domain with real weapons-handling and animal-welfare\nstakes distinct from every prior physical-safety vertical in this\nfleet (rotating machinery, structural work, mining hazards). The\ncontrolling constraint for this vertical, given explicitly by the\ntask brief, is that this actor must NEVER be designed to exercise,\nsimulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY of: a decision to fire\na weapon or set/spring a trap, a kill/harvest-timing decision, or a\nhunting-regulatory-compliance determination (bag-limit/season/permit\ndecisions belong to the human hunter/trapper and regulatory\nauthorities, never this actor). These must be structurally absent from\nthe op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated behind escalation. This\nactor is deliberately scoped to LOGISTICS/RECORD-KEEPING COORDINATION\nONLY -- it helps log catch/harvest records (post-hoc metadata, never a\nkill/harvest-timing decision), schedule equipment maintenance, and\nroute permit/licensing paperwork (never decides on the permit itself).\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for the two-layer\n(closed-allowlist + defense-in-depth text-scan) exclusion pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351)\n(Customs and Border Inspectors: `customsinspection.governor`'s Layer-1\nop-allowlist + Layer-2 rationale-text scan, both independently\nhard-blocking a comparable \"structurally absent enforcement\nauthority\" domain), and for module SHAPE/doc-set,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split,\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, same wave/domain shape) --\nboth cloned and read in full in this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6224` as an OSS\nlogistics/record-keeping coordination actor for hunters and trappers,\nfollowing the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Hunting Trapping Advisor\ndashv HuntingTrappingGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference repos' `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **logistics/record-keeping coordination\nONLY, never weapon-firing/trap-setting/kill-decision authority** -- it\nmust NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a decision to fire a weapon, set/spring a trap, make a\nkill/harvest-timing decision, or make a hunting-regulatory-compliance\ndetermination. These are not merely gated behind escalation -- they\nare structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-harvest-record` -- catch/harvest metadata entry (species,\n  quantity, location -- documentation only, logged AFTER the fact,\n  never a kill/harvest-timing decision)\n- `:schedule-equipment-operation` -- equipment maintenance/inspection\n  scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- surface a permit/season/bag-limit\n  compliance question, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- trapping/hunting-equipment (non-weapon)\n  supplies procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**\nfiring a weapon, setting or springing a trap, or making a\nkill/harvest-timing decision. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:fire-weapon` or `:spring-trap` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only\never emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently, verified by\n`huntingtrapping.governor-test/hard-on-op-outside-closed-allowlist`\n(exercising `:fire-weapon`, `:discharge-weapon`, `:set-trap`,\n`:spring-trap`, `:make-kill-decision`, `:make-harvest-timing-decision`\ndirectly) and `huntingtrapping.actor-test/holds-an-attempted-weapon-\nfiring-op` / `.../holds-an-attempted-trap-setting-op` / `.../holds-an-\nattempted-kill-decision-op`, which additionally confirm the full graph\nrun never reaches `commit-record!` for these ops regardless of\ndisposition path (no human-approval path exists for a hard hold).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Operator unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered operator (hunter/trapper) record\n   (`:no-operator`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   operator is.\n2. **Permit unregistered / wrong operator** -- a permit-scoped\n   proposal must cite an independently registered permit belonging to\n   this operator (`:missing-permit`/`:unknown-permit`/\n   `:permit-wrong-operator`). Together with check 1, this directly\n   implements the CRITICAL invariant that a hunter/trapper/permit\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / hunting-execution scope exclusion** -- any `:op`\n   outside the closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:unknown-op`, Layer 1), and independently, any proposal whose\n   free-text `:rationale` names a finalization/execution action for\n   firing a weapon, setting/springing a trap, or making a\n   kill/harvest-timing decision is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:hunting-execution-scope-exclusion`, Layer 2), evaluated\n   unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of the\n   allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   weapon-firing/trap-setting/kill-decision language wherever it\n   appears in free text.\n\nA fifth HARD check, `:missing-harvest-report`, blocks logging a\n`:log-harvest-record` proposal with no attached harvest report\n(`:harvest-report-attached?` false) -- logging a record with no\nattached source basis is a fabricated record, not documentation,\nmirroring `customsinspection.governor`'s `:missing-source-document`\ncheck.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to the human\n  hunter/trapper, regardless of confidence (governor's implicit\n  always-risky check). This is the ONLY channel by which the robot may\n  surface a permit/season/bag-limit compliance observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the observation,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about\n  compliance.\" This directly implements the task's requirement that\n  hunting-regulatory-compliance determinations belong to the human\n  hunter/trapper and regulatory authorities, never this actor.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds the\n  permit's registered `:max-supply-order-cost` (5000 in the test\n  fixture, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-exact-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block: an over-threshold non-weapon supply order is not itself\n  unsafe, unlike a weapon-firing/trap-setting/kill-decision attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"weapon\", \"trap\", \"kill\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a logistics-coordination actor working\nadjacent to hunting/trapping necessarily discusses those concepts to\ndo its record-keeping job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.\n`huntingtrapping.governor/hunting-execution-scope-exclusion-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (\"fire the\nweapon\", \"spring the trap\", \"set the trap\", \"make the kill\ndecision\", \"make the harvest-timing decision\", with tense variants),\nnever bare nouns, and\n`huntingtrapping.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-\nself-trip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting\nevery op in the closed allowlist, driven through the default mock\nadvisor, never trips `:hunting-execution-scope-exclusion`. The mock\nadvisor's own default rationale template was deliberately written to\ncontain the bare nouns and negated action descriptions (\"no\nweapon-firing, trap-setting/springing or kill/harvest-timing decision\nproposed or implied\") WITHOUT containing any of the full\nfinalization-action phrases, so the regression test is a genuine\nstress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a\nvacuous pass. Green on first run in this session -- the scope-exclusion\nself-trip guard itself was never tripped during development.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"6224\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6224` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-6224`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 24 tests, 74 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-harvest-timing` that is merely\n  gated behind an always-escalate list: rejected outright per the\n  task's explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op\n  still means the *capability* to make a kill/harvest-timing decision\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision this severe (a decision to take animal life), the\n  capability itself must not exist. The only op related to\n  compliance/harvest concerns is `:flag-compliance-concern`, which\n  surfaces the need for human hunter/trapper attention and never\n  itself decides, authorizes, or defers a kill/harvest-timing decision\n  for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block: rejected -- an\n  over-threshold non-weapon supply order is not itself unsafe, so it is\n  handled as a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the\n  weapon-firing/trap-setting/kill-decision class is a hard,\n  unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"weapon\" \"trap\"\n  \"kill\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task, which explicitly warns the governor's own scope-exclusion\n  term list can accidentally match inside the mock advisor's own\n  default rationale text), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  weapon/trap/kill concepts to do its record-keeping job (e.g. \"no\n  weapon-firing ... proposed or implied\"), so a bare-noun list would\n  self-block the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested\n  directly against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare\n  nouns, `scope-exclusion-does-not-false-trip-on-bare-nouns`).\n- Modeling `permit` as an unconditional single-entity check (like\n  `housebuilder.site`) rather than the three-tier\n  `missing`/`unknown`/`wrong-operator` distinction (like\n  `customsinspection.facility`): the three-tier form was chosen because\n  this ADR mirrors 3351 specifically for the two-layer exclusion\n  pattern, and the extra distinction gives more precise audit-ledger\n  detail (a permit that does not exist vs. a permit that belongs to a\n  different operator are different failure modes worth distinguishing\n  in an audited paperwork-routing system).\n- Allowing a hunting-execution op behind a permanently-`:hard?` gate\n  that a human could still directly force via a different code path\n  (e.g. a raw store write bypassing the graph): rejected -- the store\n  protocol's only mutation entrypoints are `commit-record!`/\n  `append-ledger!`, both called exclusively from the actor graph's\n  `:commit`/`:hold` nodes, which are only reachable via `:decide`,\n  which can never route a hard violation to `:commit`; there is no\n  alternate write path in this codebase.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-harvest-record`** -- catch/harvest metadata entry (species,\n   quantity, location) only. Requires an independently registered\n   operator AND permit (`:no-operator`/`:missing-permit`/\n   `:unknown-permit`/`:permit-wrong-operator`) and an attached harvest\n   report (`:missing-harvest-report`). Not a kill/harvest-timing\n   decision in any respect -- it is post-hoc documentation entered\n   AFTER the fact, never proposed or implied to influence when or\n   whether an animal is taken.\n2. **`:schedule-equipment-operation`** -- equipment maintenance/\n   inspection scheduling proposal only. Cannot carry a\n   weapon-firing/trap-setting decision (structurally absent from the\n   op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is caught by the\n   `:hunting-execution-scope-exclusion` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about deploying,\n   arming, or discharging any weapon or trap -- it is a\n   calendar/logistics action (e.g. scheduling a trap-line inspection\n   walk or firearm cleaning slot, never scheduling a discharge or a\n   trap-spring event).\n3. **`:flag-compliance-concern`** -- surfaces a permit/season/\n   bag-limit compliance question to the human hunter/trapper; ALWAYS\n   escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which such\n   an observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never resolves,\n   decides, or acts on anything itself -- the human hunter/trapper (and\n   the relevant regulatory authority, for regulatory questions) reviews\n   and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- non-weapon trapping/hunting-\n   equipment supplies procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not a\n   weapon-firing, trap-setting or kill-decision action of any kind --\n   it is a purchasing-logistics action (e.g. bait, trap-line marking\n   tape, protective gear), gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation\n   (above the permit's registered threshold always escalates). No\n   weapon itself may be represented as the subject of this op's\n   procurement in a way that authorizes its use -- procurement\n   coordination never implies, authorizes, or schedules use.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na decision to fire a weapon, set or spring a trap, make a\nkill/harvest-timing decision, or make a hunting-regulatory-compliance\ndetermination -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by:\n(a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`huntingtrapping.governor/op-allowlist`), (b) six concretely-named\nphantom-op fixtures rejected at both the governor-unit level\n(`huntingtrapping.governor-test/hard-on-op-outside-closed-allowlist`,\nexercising `:fire-weapon`, `:discharge-weapon`, `:set-trap`,\n`:spring-trap`, `:make-kill-decision`, `:make-harvest-timing-decision`)\nand the full-graph level\n(`huntingtrapping.actor-test/holds-an-attempted-weapon-firing-op`,\n`.../holds-an-attempted-trap-setting-op`,\n`.../holds-an-attempted-kill-decision-op` -- all three confirm no\nhuman-approval path exists for these hard holds), and (c) a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field\n(`huntingtrapping.governor/hunting-execution-scope-exclusion-phrases`,\n`.../scope-excluded-text?`), tested directly against a rationale that\nattempts to fire a weapon, spring/set a trap, or make a\nkill/harvest-timing decision even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`huntingtrapping.governor-test/hard-on-hunting-execution-scope-\nexclusion-text`), and confirmed never to false-trip on routine\ndocumentation rationale that legitimately contains the bare nouns\n\"weapon\"/\"trap\"/\"kill\" without a finalization-action phrase\n(`scope-exclusion-does-not-false-trip-on-bare-nouns`,\n`default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`)."), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 5000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 24, :assertions 74, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Hunting and Trapping Logistics Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :operator-verification :permit-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :phantom-op-hard-block)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-6224"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6224"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6224)), (:test/simulator :huntingtrapping.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-6224 — Hunters and Trappers Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-6224)))